# Migrations are plain SQL, and the guarantees are structural

Most of this schema's correctness lives in things an ORM migration generator does not model:
row-level security policies, `EXCLUDE USING gist` constraints, generated columns, partial unique
indexes and column-level grants. Auto-generation round-trips those into something subtly different
or silently drops them. Tool is `node-pg-migrate`; every `up` has an explicit `down`.

## Row-level security — `FORCE`, or the owner bypasses it

```sql
ALTER TABLE patients ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE patients FORCE  ROW LEVEL SECURITY;   -- without this the owning role skips every policy

CREATE POLICY clinic_isolation ON patients
  USING (clinic_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.clinic_id', true), '')::uuid);
```

The application connects as a role that does **not** own the tables.

## BR-02 — the database prevents double booking, not the application

Between a `SELECT` that finds the chair free and the `INSERT` that books it, another transaction can
do the same. Only a constraint closes that window.

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;

ALTER TABLE appointments
  ADD CONSTRAINT chair_not_double_booked
  EXCLUDE USING gist (chair_id WITH =, during WITH &&)
  WHERE (chair_id IS NOT NULL AND state NOT IN ('cancelled','no_show'));
```

## Append-only means revoked, not conventional

`audit_log` (BR-05) and the recovered-revenue ledger (FR-7.4.5) must survive a fully compromised
application. Corrections are compensating negative rows, never edits.

```sql
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON audit_log FROM app_rw;
GRANT  INSERT, SELECT ON audit_log TO app_rw;
```

## Masking is a grant, not a view helper (BR-05, FR-2.3)

```sql
REVOKE SELECT (phone_e164)   ON patients FROM app_reception;
GRANT  SELECT (phone_masked) ON patients TO   app_reception;
```

Store `phone_masked` as a generated column so FR-2.3 holds even against SQL injection.
